<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muslim ibn al-Hajjaj</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 875</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Imam Muslim, Abū al-Ḥusayn Muslim ibn al-Ḥajjāj</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muslim ibn al-Hajjaj, commonly known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> Muslim, was an Islamic scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> in the Abbasid province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. His date of birth is uncertain, usually given as 202, 204 or 206 AH, and he died in May 875. Of Arab origin, of the tribe of Banu Qushayr, he studied in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> before settling in Nishapur, where he became a lifelong friend of al-Bukhari. In the mid-9th century he composed Sahih Muslim, a collection of hadith he considered entirely sahih; it is one of the six canonical books of hadith in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> and, with Sahih al-Bukhari, one of the two most authentic.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 9th century scholars collected and evaluated them; the collections of al-Bukhari and <b>Muslim ibn al-Hajjaj</b> are the most respected among Sunnis, and the Shia have their own collections, which include the sayings of the imams.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li></ul></section>
